==================================================================
DAY 14 - PART B: LAB (CHECKPOINT PROJECT)
Zero-downtime update under fire
Time: 60-90 minutes
==================================================================

GOAL
----
Run your own app (YOURUSER/myapp) with 3 replicas, update it from v1
to v2 with zero downtime while pods are being killed, then roll back.


STEP 1: Build and push v2 of your app
-------------------------------------
cd ~/k8s-journey/myapp
Edit app.py: change "Hello from v1!" to "Hello from v2!"
docker build -t YOURUSER/myapp:v2 .
docker push YOURUSER/myapp:v2
(Podman: podman build -t docker.io/YOURUSER/myapp:v2 -f Containerfile . && podman push docker.io/YOURUSER/myapp:v2)


STEP 2: Write the manifests yourself
------------------------------------
mkdir -p ~/k8s-journey/manifests/week2/checkpoint && cd $_

Create checkpoint.yaml containing:
  - a Namespace "week2"
  - a Deployment "myapp" in week2:
      replicas 3, image YOURUSER/myapp:v1, containerPort 5000,
      RollingUpdate with maxSurge 1 and maxUnavailable 0,
      label app=myapp, revisionHistoryLimit 5
  - a Service "myapp" in week2, port 80 -> targetPort 5000,
    selector app=myapp

Hint for the Service:
  k create service clusterip myapp --tcp=80:5000 -n week2 --dry-run=client -o yaml
  (then change the selector to app: myapp)

k apply -f checkpoint.yaml
k get all -n week2


STEP 3: Start a traffic generator
---------------------------------
k run traffic -n week2 --image=busybox:1.36 -- sh -c 'while true; do wget -qO- -T 1 myapp || echo FAIL; sleep 0.2; done'
k logs -f traffic -n week2

You should see responses from different pod names (load balancing).


STEP 4: Start a "chaos" loop that kills pods
--------------------------------------------
In another terminal:
  while true; do
    POD=$(kubectl get pod -n week2 -l app=myapp -o name | shuf -n1)
    echo "killing $POD"
    kubectl delete -n week2 $POD --wait=false
    sleep 15
  done

(Every 15 seconds it deletes one random myapp pod.)


STEP 5: Update to v2 during chaos
---------------------------------
Update the image in checkpoint.yaml to v2, then:
  k annotate deploy/myapp -n week2 kubernetes.io/change-cause="v2" --overwrite
  k apply -f checkpoint.yaml
  k rollout status deploy/myapp -n week2

Watch the traffic log switch from v1 to v2. Count FAIL lines.


STEP 6: Roll back
-----------------
k rollout undo deploy/myapp -n week2
k rollout status deploy/myapp -n week2
k rollout history deploy/myapp -n week2

Traffic log shows v1 again. Stop the chaos loop (Ctrl+C).
Remember to put v1 back in checkpoint.yaml so file = cluster.


STEP 7: Success criteria
------------------------
[ ] 3 replicas running from your own YAML file
[ ] Pods recreated automatically when killed
[ ] Updated v1 -> v2 with 0 (or very few) FAILs
[ ] Rolled back to v1
[ ] k diff -f checkpoint.yaml shows no meaningful difference
[ ] Everything deleted and recreated from the file in under 2 minutes:
      k delete ns week2 && k apply -f checkpoint.yaml


STEP 8: Commit
--------------
cd ~/k8s-journey && git add . && git commit -m "week2 complete"
